 single day that children are not at  school is a tragedy and I don't say that  because of the impact that has on the  economy because their parents can go to  work I say that as someone who  passionately believes in the power of  Education to provide opportunities for  people that's why I got into politics in  the first place and so that's why this  is important but we ought we are  grappling with a situation where we're  trying to suppress the spread of a virus  we want our children to come back but we  need to make sure that that's going to  not make things worse which is why we've  taken the measured and responsible steps  that we have we had a plan that clearly  set out we would like to bring some  classes back off on June  first that's what we were able to do and  we met that plan we fully intend to  reopen our schools for everybody in  September and in the meantime we have  provided extra support with laptops and  Internet connections who those who  didn't have them we've created a  considerable amount of new online  resources as well and I pay tribute to  all the teachers schools had teachers  who have done a fantastic job in  difficult circumstances to do the best  that they can for their pupils you say  it said you think it's a tragedy and you  say that you know you deeply care about  the inequality that you're worried about  but at the same time you know perhaps  the most important weapon in our arsenal  against inequality is schools and  millions of children and not at school  in the classroom and may not go back to  a September at best you know I mean you  might be okay if you go to a private  school where class sizes are below 15  you might be okay if your parents can  afford to pay for private tuition in  these months where you're not at school  but what about all the other children  are we not risking a lost generation  here well that's why it's very important  that we continue the work that we've  done on suppressing the virus so we can  get our lives back to normal as well and  you're right this is one of the costs of  the lockdown we've talked previously in  the interview about the economic cost of  the lockdown this is an this is another  impact that it has it also has an impact  on people's health both in the short  term with those who are not seeking  treatment right now and those who will  have longer term health conditions as a  result of what happens to them through  the lockdown you know these are all the  complicated issues that we are grappling  with trying to balance balance these  things out I think well it when it comes  to schools there are countries like  Italy and Ireland for example who are  not bringing back any pupils until  September you know other countries like  Spain the vast majority will not come  back until September so yeah I think  where we are is a reasonable and  measured approach but I completely  understand and sympathize that we would  all like to see our children at school  but that's all we've provided all these  other things and we'll keep doing so to  make sure that our kids get the support  they need although at the same time  there's many other countries Denmark  Austria Germany Norway where they have  reopen schools and they haven't seen a  significant rise in infections either it  does make me feel as well there's been  you know Herculean effort to ramp up  capacity in the NHS you've led an  unprecedented economic  intervention and very expensive 1t to  directly pay people's wages why is the  same urgency not been shown to get  children back to school should the  Education Secretary have led a national  mobilization in the same way I think the  education secretary should take  significant action we've created a brand  new online Academy providing I think the  downloads and the interaction with that  have been in the millions together with  other institutions have done the same  but that someone would put in place it's  been very well users have provided a lot  of support to children teachers and  schools up and down the country also  made sure that our most vulnerable  children do have access to online  resources through delivering I think a  hundred thousand laptops and internet  connections to those who didn't have  them something that's wrong to say that  nothing has been done he's very focused  on this issue and he's been working  exceptionally hard as has all of us with  the the teaching unions and with schools  to make sure we've got a plan that is  deliverable has said we delivered on  that plan to bring back especially our  youngest children on the 1st of June as  we said and you did mention some  countries that have moved faster than uh  I acknowledge that but as I said there  are also countries that are moving  slower than us on this issue so I do  think you know we are where we are is  reasonable and sensible and has to be  taken in the round of everything that  we've done as well okay and I just came  to talk about a couple of things that  you've done in your own Treasury support  schemes government gyeom's updated on  Friday which seems to say that after  June the 10th if you're vulnerable and  binds to shield by your GP you can't be  furloughed unless you've been furloughed  before now Citizens Advice estimates  over 70% of those people shielding  haven't previously been furloughed so  these people may have to choose between  following the advice and keeping their  jobs are you going to be addressing this  well I think there's two different  things here I mean the furloughing  scheme which we've put in place was to  protect people's employment which I  think it has done over a million  businesses of benefiting and around 9  million people are having their jobs  protected through this crisis as a  result of that scheme so I think it's  something that has made enormous  difference to people's security you know  when it comes to people who are  shielding we've put in place a  comprehensive set of support for them  especially delivered through local  government to whom I'm very grateful for  in the local government secretary has  worked very high  this to make sure they get the support  and the resources they need whether it's  food deliveries or other community  support to help them through this and  for those from the beginning of this  crisis that have been either isolating  or otherwise have access to our welfare  system when we made changes to that  right at the beginning of this crisis to  ensure that it worked for all those  people because these are essentially our  new situations that we find ourselves in  okay and now I'll be honest I was  actually planning on asking this  question but since we announced you were  coming on the show I've been inundated  with messages on social media from  people who say that they fall through  the cracks  of the schemes that have been put in  place by and the government so people  have recently gone self-employed piyu  freelancers do you accept that there are  potentially millions of people who  aren't getting any support from the  government at this really difficult time  and would you apologize to them I'd say  what Sophie I already did talk directly  to to all of them and I happy to say so  again I know not everyone has been  helped in exactly the way that they  would have wanted through this crisis I  fully acknowledge that and and of course  I'm sorry we weren't able to do that but  those were reasons that those were  decisions that were taken with very  specific reasons you have to remember we  were dealing with something that was  hitting us at incredible speed and with  such severity that there was a premium  on doing things that could help as many  people as possible as quickly as  possible it could actually be delivered  it's no point coming up with lots of  neat ideas but they would have taken  months and months to implement so you  know that's why we did what we did we've  been able to help as I said 9 million  jobs protected through the furloughing  scheme two and a half million people  benefiting from the self-employed scheme  yeah and you know close to a million  businesses benefiting from various  discounted loans business race holidays  cash grants now so I'm proud of all of  that I acknowledge some people will feel  that they didn't quite get what they  want but we've also strengthened our  welfare system considerably as well so  I'm confident that across the piece an  enormous amount of support has been  provided and if we couldn't do it in the  way that you wanted it was because it  would have meant that we couldn't have  done all these other things which were  really important given the situation we  were facing I  okay I mean that does certainly sound  like there's a degree of honesty there  and these the kind of things that you  could look at an emergency budget  perhaps next month well I think at this  point I want to try and look forward  rather look backwards and we've put  these things in place to help bridge  through the crisis and as you as we  started off talking about we're now  looking forward to trying to reopen our  country get our economy going again we  can do that because we've made progress  against the virus we've met all our five  tests so the priority now has got to be  driving that recovery rather than  looking backwards that starts tomorrow  with our shops reopening hopefully when  we get into July then we can get our  pubs our restaurants our hospitality  sector oath and get those millions of  people back to the jobs they love get  give those businesses of proper chance  of actually surviving through this those  are all the things I'm focused on now  then we've got to look forward and we  set out an ambitious agenda at budget as  well about investing in our regions  making sure we drive growth and  productivity through infrastructure  investment I'm keen to get off get on  with that and get back to that so that's  what I'll be looking forward to in the  coming months okay now I am being told  to my ear that we've run out of time I'm  just gonna get one last question in if  that's okay you did a star turn at the  1922 committee of conservative that  benches you've got a pretty strong  social media game whether it's been  pictured in hoodies or drinking  Yorkshire tea you're the bookies  favorite to be the next Conservative  leader you tempted by the top job you  know I I think you saw me I think I've  been in this job for just a week or two  when we saw each other enormous amount  has happened enormous amount has  happened since and it's a great  privilege to have this job you know I'm  very grateful to the Prime Minister for  putting both his confidence and his  trust in me you know especially given  the the crisis that we're facing and I  very much hope I can just keep doing the  job that I've done and keep on to  provide support to people and best I can  so at the moment we're all pulling  together a one team and that's what  we're all focused on collectively  together okay thank you very much  Ritchie scenic the transferor thank you
 

